{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Abraham was old, advanced in days.</b> Scripture returned to say this in order to make known the cause for which he adjured his servant. For he saw himself very old and reasoned in his heart that if he sent to the land of his kindred, perhaps before the messenger returned he would depart to his eternal home. Therefore he adjured his servant that Isaac would follow his counsel, for he is the one who rules over all that is his, so that he would not take a wife for himself from the daughters of Canaan. And in Bereshit Rabbah (48:19) they said: here, old age that has deterioration in it; and further on, old age that has no deterioration in it. They intend to explain that \"advanced in days\" (בָּא בַּיָּמִים) begins the days of old age, for \"coming\" (בָּאִים) indicates ongoing time, like \"who come in through these gates\" (הַבָּאִים בַּשְּׁעָרִים הָאֵלֶּה) (Jeremiah 7:2). And here it states that he was very old, that he had already advanced in days, like \"your brother came with deceit\" (בָּא אָחִיךָ בְּמִרְמָה) (Genesis 27:35)." "<b>And HaShem had blessed Abraham with everything.</b> In wealth and possessions and honor and length of days and children — and this is the entire desire of man (Ibn Ezra). And Scripture mentioned this to say that he was complete in everything, lacking nothing except that he should see children for his son who would inherit his excellence and honor, and therefore he longed for this.\n\nAnd our Rabbis have a wondrous matter in this. They said (Bava Batra 16b): \"And YHWH had blessed Abraham with everything\" — Rabbi Meir says: he had no daughter; Rabbi Yehudah says: he had a daughter; Others say: he had a daughter and her name was Bakol. Rabbi Meir interpreted that Abraham had no daughter, and this was a blessing to him, for he could not give her in marriage except to the accursed sons of Canaan, and if he sent her to his land she too would worship idolatry like them, for a woman is under the authority of her husband, and Abraham would not want his legitimate seed from Sarah his wife to go forth from the land, and even more so that she would worship idolatry. And Rabbi Yehudah interpreted that he did have a daughter — for the Merciful One did not withhold even a daughter from him (ibid. 141a) — and this is the blessing of \"in everything,\" for he had everything that people desire, lacking nothing.\n\nAnd the Others came and mentioned the name of the daughter. But in truth the intent of the Others and their dispute with Rabbi Yehudah is only to inform us of the name of this daughter. Heaven forbid that they should reduce the blessing of Abraham — which is great and all-encompassing in this regard — by having Scripture say that HaShem blessed him with one daughter whose name was such-and-such. Rather, the Others innovated in the interpretation of this verse a very profound matter, and they darshened in it a secret from the secrets of the Torah. They said that \"bakol\" (בַּכֹּל) alludes to a great matter, namely that the Holy One, blessed be He, has a middah called \"Kol\" (כֹּל, \"Everything\"), because it is the foundation of everything. And of it is said (Isaiah 44:24): \"I am YHWH, who makes kol.\" And thus it is stated (Ecclesiastes 5:8): \"And the advantage of the land is in bakol\" — he says that the advantage and great goodness of the land that overflows upon all who come into the world is because in bakol it is. And it is the eighth middah of the thirteen middot. And another middah is called \"Bat\" (בַּת, \"Daughter\"), emanated from it, and through it He governs the Kol; and it is the Beit Din of the Holy One, blessed be He, alluded to in the word \"and YHWH\" (וַה') in every place (Bereshit Rabbah 51:2). And it is what is called \"Kallah\" (כַּלָּה, \"Bride\") in the book of Shir haShirim, because it is included within the Kol. And it is what the Sages designate by the name \"Kenesset Yisrael\" in many places, because it is the assembly of the Kol.\n\nAnd this middah was to Abraham like a daughter, for he was the man of lovingkindness and would conduct himself through it. Therefore the Others said that this blessing of being blessed with bakol does not allude to whether he begot a daughter from Sarah his wife or did not beget one; rather, it alludes to a great matter, that He blessed him in the middah that is within the middah of Kol, and therefore it too is called \"Kol,\" in the manner of \"for My name is within him\" (כִּי שְׁמִי בְּקִרְבּוֹ) (Exodus 23:21). And behold, he is blessed in heaven and on earth, and therefore it says: \"by YHWH, the Elohim of heaven and the Elohim of the earth.\"\n\nAnd this matter is found among our Rabbis alluded to in the Aggadot in many places, like the matter they said in Midrash Chazita (Shir haShirim Rabbah 3:21): Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai asked Rabbi Eliezer ben Rabbi Yosi: Is it possible that you heard from your father what is \"in the crown with which his mother crowned him\" (בָּעֲטָרָה שֶׁעִטְּרָה לוֹ אִמּוֹ) (Song of Songs 3:11)? He said to him: Yes. He said to him: How? He said to him: A parable — to a king who had an only daughter and loved her more than enough, and he called her \"my daughter.\" He did not cease loving her until he called her \"my sister.\" And he did not cease loving her until he called her \"my mother.\" So too, at first the Holy One, blessed be He, loved Israel and called them \"my daughter,\" as it is written (Psalms 45:11): \"Hear, O daughter, and see.\" And He did not cease loving them until He called them \"my sister,\" as is stated (Song of Songs 5:2): \"Open for me, my sister, my beloved.\" And He did not cease loving them until He called them \"my mother,\" as is stated (Isaiah 51:4): \"Listen to me, my people (עַמִּי, ul'umi), and my nation\" — as \"my mother\" (לְאִמִּי) it is written. Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai stood and kissed him on his head. He said to him: If I had come only to hear this one thing from your mouth, it would suffice.\n\nAnd this is explained in the midrash of Rabbi Nechunya ben haKanah (Sefer haBahir 22). They said on the verse: \"I am YHWH, who makes kol, who stretches out the heavens alone, who spreads out the earth from with Me\" (אָנֹכִי ה' עֹשֶׂה כֹּל נֹטֶה שָׁמַיִם לְבַדִּי רֹקַע הָאָרֶץ מֵאִתִּי) (Isaiah 44:24) — I, when I planted this tree to delight in it the whole world, and I spread out in it the Kol, and I called its name Kol, for everything depends on it and everything emerges from it and everything needs it, and in it they gaze and for it they hope, and from there souls fly forth. Alone was I when I made it, and no angel grows great over it to say \"I preceded you.\" Also at the time I spread out My earth, in it I planted and rooted this tree, and I rejoiced together and I rejoiced in them — who is with Me, that I revealed this secret of Mine? Thus far. And further there it is explained: What is \"In rage, remember the womb\" (בְּרֹגֶז רַחֵם תִּזְכֹּר) (Habakkuk 3:2)? He said: At the time your children sinned against you and you grew angry with them — \"remember the womb (רַחֵם תִּזְכֹּר).\" And what is \"remember the womb\"? Remember the one of whom it says \"I love You, YHWH, my strength\" (אֶרְחָמְךָ ה' חִזְקִי) (Psalms 18:2), and You gave him this middah, which is the Shekhinah of Israel. And remember his son who inherited it, and You gave him this middah, which is the Shekhinah of Israel, as it is written (I Kings 5:26): \"And YHWH gave wisdom to Solomon.\" And remember their father Abraham, as it is written: \"Seed of Abraham, My beloved (אוֹהֲבִי)\" (Isaiah 41:8). And \"In the midst of years make it known\" (וּבְקֶרֶב שָׁנִים תּוֹדִיעַ) (Habakkuk 3:2).\n\nAnd from where was there a daughter to Abraham? Behold, it is written: \"And YHWH had blessed Abraham with bakol,\" and it is written (Isaiah 43:7): \"Everyone who is called by My name, and for My glory I created him.\" This blessing was His daughter — or was it not so but rather His mother? Behold, His daughter it was. A parable: To a master who had a servant, complete and blameless before him, and tested him with several trials, and he stood through all of them. The master said: What shall I give this servant, or what shall I do for him? Rather, I will command my elder brother to counsel him and guard him and honor him. The servant went about with the elder brother and learned his ways. The brother loved him and called him \"my beloved,\" as it is written: \"Seed of Abraham, My beloved.\" He said: What shall I give him, or what shall I do for him? Behold, I have made a fine vessel, and in it are fine jewels, the like of which none exist, and they are the treasure of kings — I shall give it to him and he shall merit in my place. This is what is written: \"And YHWH had blessed Abraham with bakol.\" Thus far.\n\nAnd if you understand what I have written, you will know the statement of the accursed women who said: \"But since we ceased burning incense to the Queen of Heaven, we have lacked everything (כֹל)\" (Jeremiah 44:18) — and why is the word written missing the aleph (כל instead of כל)? And you will discern many sealed matters in the Torah and in Scripture. And if only this one who prides himself in his secrets would know this, his lips would be taught to stop mocking the words of our Rabbis. Therefore I have written this to close the mouth of the speakers against the righteous with might.\n\nn: The closing phrase עָתָק is rendered \"with might\"; it may also convey \"excellence\" or \"force,\" and the exact sense is uncertain. The word חַסְרַת in the reference to Jeremiah means \"defective\" (i.e., written without the aleph). The citation from Sefer haBahir is paragraph 22 in standard editions. The closing allusion to one who mocks the Sages appears to be directed at a specific critic, left unidentified as in the original."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :notes {0 "The phrase לִכְלוּכִית, which appears in Bereshit Rabbah as לכלוכית, refers to physical decline or deterioration in old age; rendered literally."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:34:41.023027492Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}